target: cmd/looper/main.go
verify: go test ./cmd/looper/ -count=1 -run 'TestPushCheck$'
expect: a refusal exited
why:    a refusal that exits 0 makes `looper push-check … && git push` push on push:false. The friction log records exactly that twice (2026-10-16, 2026-10-20): the JSON said push false and the chained push ran anyway. Exit 0 was once the contract, so that a refusal could not be mistaken for the tool failing; Nathan chose a DISTINCT code instead (2026-10-21) — 3 with the JSON still printed — which keeps that distinction and stops the chain. Replaces push-check-refusal-exits-non-zero, which guarded the old contract.
--- old
	// After the write: a refusal carries its answer.
	if !res.Push {
		return errPushRefused
	}
--- new
	// After the write: a refusal carries its answer.
